<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helots</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as helot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The helots were a subjugated group who formed most of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia, the territories ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>; tied to the land, they farmed it to support the Spartan citizens. Ancient writers disagreed on their status: Critias called them &quot;slaves to the utmost&quot;, Pollux put them &quot;between free men and slaves&quot;. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> declared war on them every year, so that Spartans could kill them without religious pollution. Some won freedom by military service, like the 700 who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a> in 424 BCE; freed helots were called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neodamodes">neodamodeis</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ancient authors gave several accounts of where the name came from. Hellanicus linked it to the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helos">Helos</a>, in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and Pausanias repeats that its inhabitants were the first slaves of the Spartan state. Modern linguists connect it with a root meaning to be captured. Antiochus of Syracuse wrote that Lacedaemonians who did not join the expedition were judged slaves and named helots, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus of Cyme</a> made them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioikoi</a> of Helos, reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> after a failed revolt.</p><p>Conquest was clearly part of the story: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a>, conquered in the Messenian Wars of the 8th century BCE, became in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> a synonym for helots. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a> said the earliest helots descended from Achaeans whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> had conquered, though not all Achaeans were reduced to helotry; the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyclae">Amyclae</a> kept a special status.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Helots were assigned to citizens to do domestic work or to farm their klēroi, the plots into which Messenia was first divided. They handed over a fixed share of the harvest and kept the surplus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> gives the amount as 70 medimnoi of barley for a man and 12 for a woman, while the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrtaeus">Tyrtaeus</a> speaks of half the produce; scholars debate both. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia were fertile, so helots could often live fairly well. They lived in families, and some owned property: in 425 BCE some had their own boats, and in 223 BCE 6,000 bought their freedom for 500 drachmas each.</p><p>Spartan treatment was harsh. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> says that most Spartan institutions were designed for security against the helots. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> reports that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> declared war on them each year, and Myron of Priene that they received beatings regardless of wrongdoing. Some modern scholars read these accounts as ideological scorn rather than daily cruelty. Helots also followed Spartans as servants and light troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> counts seven for each Spartan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Helots could be freed, especially for military service. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> tells that freedom was promised to those who carried supplies to the besieged Spartans on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sphacteria">Sphacteria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> that more than 6,000 volunteered when the Thebans invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>. The 700 helots who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a> in 424 BCE were freed as the Brasidians, and freed helots were called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neodamodes">neodamodeis</a>.</p><p>The earthquake of 464 BCE brought a major revolt, in which helots and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioikoi</a> of Thouria and Aithaia, mostly of Messenian ancestry, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ithome">Mount Ithome</a>. Plutarch says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crypteia">Crypteia</a> and other ill-treatment began after it. The Athenians later settled helots at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a> and garrisoned Pylos with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a>. The helots were largely liberated by the Theban commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> after the Theban–Spartan War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helots” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thasos defected in 465 BCE, after Athens founded the colony of Amphipolis on the Strymon river near its mines of Mt. Pangaion; Sparta, facing the largest <b>helot</b> revolt in its history, refused it aid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Full citizens formed a privileged class, while the land was worked by dependent rural populations, called by various names in Cretan sources, who were bound to the soil and excluded from political life; Aristotle compared them to the <b>helots</b> of Sparta, though he noted they were less rebellious.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Spartan society rested on a sharp division between a small body of full citizens, the Spartiates or homoioi, peers, the free but subordinate perioikoi, and the <b>helots</b>, state-owned serfs of Laconia and Messenia who worked the land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trained and led by Epaminondas and Pelopidas, with the Sacred Band of Thebes, the Theban army won at Leuctra in 371 BCE, then carried its arms into the Peloponnesus, freeing many <b>helots</b>, and sent expeditions to Thessaly and Macedon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>helots</b> of Sparta belonged to the state rather than to individual citizens; they lived in families and kept part of the harvest, yet each year the ephors declared war on them, so that killing one brought no religious pollution, and at Plataea in 479 BCE there were,…</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>Around the citizens lived the free perioikoi in their own towns and several in-between groups: inferiors who had lost citizenship, foster brothers raised beside Spartiate boys, and freed <b>helots</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
